<PageLayout type="article" enOnly published="2026-10-23" title="Weighted vs Unweighted GPA: Differences Explained" description="Weighted vs unweighted GPA explained: how each is calculated, what scale they use, which one colleges look at, and how to find yours with a free calculator." path="/weighted-vs-unweighted-gpa/" heading="Weighted vs Unweighted GPA">
  <p>Most high school transcripts show two GPAs. They answer different questions, and knowing the difference helps you read your own record the way a college does.</p>

  <h2>Unweighted GPA</h2>
  <p>An unweighted GPA ignores how hard a class is. An A is 4.0 whether it is in Regular, Honors or AP. The scale tops out at 4.0, so it shows how consistently you earned top grades.</p>

  <h2>Weighted GPA</h2>
  <p>A weighted GPA adds bonus points for advanced classes. Typical rules are +0.5 for Honors and +1.0 for AP, IB and Dual Enrollment, so the scale commonly runs to 5.0 (some schools go higher). It rewards taking harder courses.</p>

  <h2>Side-by-side example</h2>
  <p>A student earns an A in AP Calculus, a B in Honors English and an A in Regular Art (all 1 credit).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unweighted:</strong> (4.0 + 3.0 + 4.0) ÷ 3 = 3.67</li>
    <li><strong>Weighted:</strong> (5.0 + 3.5 + 4.0) ÷ 3 = 4.17</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which one do colleges use?</h2>
  <p>Practices vary. Many admissions offices recalculate GPA on their own scale, often using core academic classes only, and read it alongside your course rigor listed in the school profile. Scholarships and class rank may use either number, so check each program's rules.</p>

  <h2>Which should you track?</h2>
  <p>Track both. The unweighted GPA shows performance on a common scale, and the weighted GPA shows the benefit of rigor. Our <a href="/#calculator">high school GPA calculator</a> shows both at once, per year and cumulative.</p>

